# RiskGate production rule set: the model plus rules (experiment 8,
# "model plus rules").
#
# Evaluation follows Radar's documented order: allow rules first (an allowed
# payment skips block and review), then block, then review. A payment no
# rule matches is allowed. Shadow rules run on every payment and are logged,
# never enforced; GET /v1/rules/shadow compares them with their backtest.
#
# Tuned on the VALIDATION month only (month 4, 83,571 payments, 2,850 fraud,
# $462,548 fraud and $10,710,804 legitimate dollars), on the scored table
# data/cache/table_scored_real.rgt, with the backtester's threshold sweep and
# incremental backtests. The test month was not looked at. The log, with
# every candidate tried, is results/rules_default/TUNING.md.
#
# Caveats that apply to every number below: risk_score's isotonic
# calibration was fitted on this same month and the model early-stopped on
# it, so validation numbers are optimistic. "Incremental" means what the rule
# changes given all the other live rules in this file (a leave-one-out
# backtest), which is what the rule is worth, as opposed to what it matches.
#
# The whole set on validation: 2,263 payments flagged (370 blocked, 1,893
# reviewed), precision 39.1%, fraud recall 31.1%, FPR 1.71%, fraud-dollar
# recall 35.7%. Of legitimate dollars, 0.36% blocked and 2.05% reviewed.
#
# Check this file with:  go run ./cmd/rulecheck -lists rules/lists.json rules/default.rules
#
# No allow rule. The starter allow rule (trusted domain, customer seen for
# 30+ days, risk_score < 20) matched 11,978 payments at 1.3% fraud but
# changed no decision: nothing else here fires below risk_score 15. The
# rules-baseline allow rule (customer seen 90+ days ago, idle 7+ days) would
# have released 7 flagged payments, 4 of them fraud. Neither helps.

# --- Block: the model is more likely right than wrong -----------------------
# risk_score is a calibrated fraud probability in percent. Blocking loses the
# payment's amount if it is legitimate and saves the amount plus a $15
# dispute fee if it is fraud, so blocking pays when p > a / (2a + 15), just
# under 50%. Validation scores sit on the calibration's steps (..., 45, 56,
# 63, ...), so any cut from 46 to 56 is the same cut here. In those dollar
# terms every step from 63 up is net positive and every step up to 45 net
# negative; the step at 56 (81 payments, 57% fraud) is -$1,199, because its
# fraud is small-ticket. Cutting at 63 instead would gain that $1,199 on
# validation; 50 is kept because it is the principled break-even and 81
# payments are too few to tune to.
# Validation: 370 matched, 71.4% fraud; incremental (review -> block) 370,
# $69,189 fraud and $38,299 legitimate dollars (0.36% of legitimate dollars).
block if :risk_score: >= 50

# --- Review: the model says one in four or more -----------------------------
# In Clearinghouse, review lets the payment through and holds the payout,
# so it costs an analyst's time and a delay, not the sale. 25 keeps every
# score step in the queue at 25% fraud or more, and with the product C rule
# below the queue is 1,893 payments a month (2.3%, about 63 a day). Validation: 2,153 matched, 39.1%; incremental 1,461 payments,
# 28.7% fraud, $77,306 fraud and $195,990 legitimate dollars.
review if :risk_score: >= 25

# --- Review: product C over $50 that the model already half-suspects -------
# Product C is 10% of payments and 38% of fraud, and nearly every product C
# payment has no billing_region, so the customer (uid) features the model
# uses elsewhere are missing there. Within risk_score 15-24, product C over
# $50 is 39% fraud, well above the review band's 25% edge. Validation: 531
# matched, 53.7%; incremental 110 payments, 39.1% fraud (43), $3,820 fraud
# and $6,746 legitimate dollars.
review if :product_code: = "C" and :amount: > 50 and :risk_score: >= 15

# Dropped after the validation backtest (incremental precision given the
# rules above, against a 25% bar for review and a net-positive bar for
# block; details in TUNING.md):
#   block  distinct_cards_per_device_24h > 5     5.8% (device_info is a model
#                                                 string, not a device)
#   block  card 1 h count >= 8 and 3x 7-day mean 6.5%
#   block  disposable domain and amount > 500    1.6%
#   review anonymous.com and amount > 300        1.3% (below the 3.4% base rate)
#   review uid amount ratio > 5 and amount > 200 1.4%
#   review C, no device_info, risk_score >= 30   0 changed (the >= 25 band
#                                                 covers it); at >= 15 it adds
#                                                 22.1% given the C rule above

# --- Shadow: candidates being measured before they go live -----------------
# Promote the product C rule's upper half from review to block. On
# validation it would move 322 reviewed payments to block, 49.4% fraud,
# $14,954 fraud against $16,743 legitimate dollars: break-even, so measure
# it on live traffic before deciding.
shadow block if :product_code: = "C" and :amount: > 50 and :risk_score: >= 25

# Discover cards the model scores 15-24. Validation: 47 incremental
# payments, 27.7% fraud (13), $4,854 fraud and $10,711 legitimate dollars.
# Just over the review bar on 13 frauds, which is too few to trust.
shadow review if :card_network: = "discover" and :risk_score: >= 15
